[Event "GBR-ch 45th"]
[Site "Leamington"]
[Date "1958.08.18"]
[Round "1.5"]
[White "Fazekas, Stefan"]
[Black "Rumens, David Edward"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "A04"]
[Annotator "Wood, BH"]
[PlyCount "72"]
[EventDate "1958.08.18"]
[EventType "swiss"]
[EventRounds "11"]
[EventCountry "GBR"]

1. Nf3 f5 2. d3 ({In my magazine Chess I published an article some months ago
on what might happen now if White were to continue} 2. e4 {This opening 1.Nf3
f5 2.e4, a gambit of fascinating interest, is, up to now, almost completely
unexplored. I have not the least doubt that Rumens has spent a tremendous lot
of his spare time analysing it before inviting 2.e4 as he obviously did in
this game- and I think Dr. Fazekas was wise to side-track it, though he could
surely have found a more aggressive move than 2.d3 - BH Wood, ILN}) 2... d6 3.
e4 e5 4. exf5 {To 'give up the centre' like this and present your opponent
with a good developing move is just pure bad strategy - BH Wood, ILN} Bxf5 5.
d4 e4 6. Nfd2 Nf6 7. Be2 Be7 8. O-O Nc6 9. f3 Nxd4 10. fxe4 Bg6 11. Bc4 d5 12.
Rxf6 Bxf6 13. Bxd5 c6 14. Bc4 Qb6 15. Kh1 O-O-O 16. a4 Rhe8 {It is usually the
prerogative of the one who has sacrificed to have the better development; but
here, White, who is the exchange down, has only two pieces in 'play' whereas
Black has six! White’s game is a shambles. He battles manfully but the
arrears are too great - BH Wood, ILN} 17. Nc3 Qc7 18. Bd3 Be5 19. Nf1 Ne6 20.
Qg4 Kb8 21. a5 Qf7 22. Be3 Bh5 23. Qf5 Nf4 24. a6 Qxf5 25. exf5 Nxd3 26. cxd3
Rxd3 27. Bd2 b6 28. h3 Bd4 29. Kh2 Be5+ 30. Kg1 Red8 31. Bg5 R8d7 32. Rc1 h6
33. Bh4 Bd4+ 34. Kh2 Bxc3 35. Bg3+ $2 Rxg3 {"and Black won" Source: BCM, Oct
1958, p250} 36. Kxg3 Be5+ {"and Black, a clear piece up, soon won" Source:
Illustrated London News, 6 September 1958 (BH Wood column)} 0-1

